# Tpad

Tpad is a small, fast text editor for plain text and source files. It provides
a GTK 4 frontend for current Linux systems, a GTK 3 compatibility frontend for
older Ubuntu releases, and a Qt 6 frontend for macOS, Windows, and Linux. Each
command-line file opens in its own editor window.

The File menu includes a Print action (`Ctrl+P`) that opens the frontend's
native system print dialog. Printer selection, print-to-file, page setup,
ranges, and other options come from the platform print service.

Tpad keeps a frontend-independent recent-file history in `~/.tpad.recent`.
Successful opens and saves move a file to the top of File → Open Recent.
Recording is enabled by default; Preferences can disable it or set a maximum
from 0 to 1,000,000 entries (default 100, with 0 meaning unlimited). Disabling
history stops updates and disables Open Recent without deleting the list.

Frequency Analysis examines the current selection, or the whole document when
nothing is selected, in a scrollable Unicode table containing Character, Code
point, Count, and Percent columns.

## Building

The complete prerequisites and commands for GTK, macOS Qt 6.11.1, Windows
Qt 6.11.1, shared deployment, and static Qt kits are in `BUILDING.md`.

For the GTK 4 build on Debian or Ubuntu, install the normal
Autotools toolchain plus these project development packages:

- `libgtk-4-dev` (GTK 4.10 or newer)
- `libgtksourceview-5-dev`
- `libspelling-1-dev`
- `libsystemd-dev`
- `gettext`, `intltool`, `libtool`, and `pkg-config`

Then build and test from the repository root:

```sh
./autogen.sh
make -j"$(nproc)"
make check
```

`./autogen.sh` regenerates the Autotools files and runs `configure`. Use
`NOCONFIGURE=1 ./autogen.sh` when only the generated build files are needed.
When the GTK 4 stack is unavailable, configure falls back to GTK 3. Install
`libgtk-3-dev`, `libgtksourceview-3.0-dev`, and `libgtkspell3-3-dev`, then run
`./autogen.sh --gtk3` to select that compatibility frontend explicitly.

The cross-platform frontend uses CMake. Qt is the default on macOS and
Windows; this explicit form selects a Qt 6.11.2 installation:

```sh
cmake -S . -B build-qt \
  -DTPAD_USE_QT=ON \
  -DTPAD_QT_PATH=/opt/Qt/6.11.2/macos \
  -DTPAD_QT_STATIC=OFF
cmake --build build-qt --parallel
```

Use a platform-appropriate Qt path. Static linking requires a separately
built static Qt kit; setting the option alone cannot convert a shared kit.

Tpad keeps its small, embedded mbedTLS hashing subset as separate source and
header files under `src/`; its provenance and license are documented in
`src/mbedtls/README.tpad` and `src/mbedtls/LICENSE`.

## Release testing and packaging

The repeatable clang-23 sanitizer, static-analyzer, Valgrind, fuzzing, GUI, and
packaging procedure is in `RELEASE_TESTING.md`. Debian metadata is under
`debian/`, and the strict Core 24 Snap recipe is `snap/snapcraft.yaml`.

Report bugs at <https://bugs.launchpad.net/tpad-project/+filebug>.

